<!--
TRIANGLE PROTOCOL (CLICK)

DESIGN INSPIRATION
Reference: https://www.orvelumm.com/lab?utm_source

This website inspired my exploration of an experimental
digital interface and an immersive visual atmosphere.
I used it as a visual reference when developing the
direction of Triangle Protocol, while adapting the
design to suit my own memory-game concept and interaction.

PROJECT CONTEXT AND INTERACTION
Triangle Protocol is a browser-based memory reconstruction game.
The player observes a sequence of highlighted triangle nodes
and then repeats the same sequence by clicking the nodes in
the correct order. The fictional security-system theme gives
this simple memory activity a clear purpose: each completed
level represents progress towards restoring access to the system.

INTERFACE STRUCTURE
I organised the interface into an introduction screen and a
separate game screen. The introduction establishes the context
before the player starts interacting. This separation is intended
to make the first step clear and avoid presenting all the game
information at once.

HTML SEMANTICS AND CONTROLS
The HTML uses semantic elements such as header, main, section,
aside and footer to organise content into meaningful areas.
Buttons are used for actions because they are interactive controls,
rather than using ordinary text elements as clickable controls.
The type="button" attribute makes the intended button behaviour
explicit.

The hidden attribute is used on the game screen and feedback
overlays so that these elements can be shown or hidden as the
interaction progresses. The JavaScript controls when the player
moves between screens and when error or success feedback becomes
visible.

ACCESSIBILITY
Accessibility was also considered. Labels such as aria-label
provide descriptions for interface elements where visual appearance
alone may not communicate their purpose. Decorative elements use
aria-hidden="true" so that they do not add unnecessary information
to the accessibility tree. The progress bar also includes ARIA
attributes to communicate the level-progress range and current value.

VISUAL ASSETS AND ATTRIBUTION
The triangle shapes are generated through inline SVG in JavaScript,
rather than loaded from an external image library. The supplied HTML
does not reference third-party image, icon or audio assets. Therefore,
there are no external media creators or media licences to attribute
for the assets shown in this version. The visual reference above is
acknowledged as inspiration for the interface direction.
-->

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Triangle Protocol</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
  <script src="script.js" defer></script>
</head>
<body>
  <!--
  SITE HEADER
  The header identifies the fictional system and displays its
  online status. Keeping this information separate from the main
  content establishes the visual identity before the player begins.
  -->
  <header class="site-header">
    <a class="logo" href="#">TRIANGLE<span>_PROTOCOL</span></a>
    <div class="system-status">
      <span class="status-dot"></span>
      SYSTEM ONLINE
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="main-container">

    <!--
    FIRST SCREEN: INTRODUCTION
    This screen introduces the memory-reconstruction concept
    before presenting the interactive game. The main heading,
    short instruction and button establish a clear starting point.
    The player must activate the system before accessing the game.
    -->
    <section class="intro-screen" id="intro-screen">
      <p class="eyebrow">MEMORY RECONSTRUCTION SYSTEM</p>

      <h1>Reconstruct the <span>sequence.</span></h1>

      <p class="intro-description">
        Observe the signal. Remember the pattern. Restore the system.
      </p>

      <!-- Decorative elements support the interface atmosphere
           without adding unnecessary information for screen readers. -->
      <div class="intro-decoration" aria-hidden="true">
        <span></span>
        <span></span>
        <span></span>
      </div>

      <p class="intro-instruction">
        A COGNITIVE MEMORY TEST // TP-07
      </p>

      <!-- The button provides the first clear Click interaction.
           Its visible label communicates the action to the player. -->
      <button class="system-unlock-button" id="system-unlock-button" type="button">
        UNLOCK THE SYSTEM
        <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </button>

      <p class="intro-footer">INITIALISE THE INTERFACE TO CONTINUE</p>
    </section>

    <!--
    SECOND SCREEN: GAME
    The hidden attribute keeps the game out of view until the
    player enters the interactive experience. This staged structure
    avoids overwhelming new players with controls at the beginning.
    -->
    <section class="game-screen" id="game-screen" hidden>
      <div class="game-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">NEURAL INTERFACE // TP-07</p>
        <h1>Memory <span>Protocol.</span></h1>
        <p class="intro-description">
          Observe the signal. Remember the pattern. Restore the system.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- The game layout groups the main interaction and its controls
           into separate areas to make the interface easier to scan. -->
      <section class="game-layout" aria-label="Triangle memory game">

        <!-- LEFT PANEL
             This panel contains the triangle matrix, game status
             information and visual feedback during interaction. -->
        <div class="matrix-panel">
          <div class="panel-top">
            <span>TRIANGLE INTERFACE</span>
            <span class="panel-indicator">● ACTIVE</span>
          </div>

          <!-- GAME STATUS HUD
               The HUD presents the current input state, signal status,
               security level and access mode. These indicators help
               players understand what the system is doing. -->
          <section class="neural-hud" aria-label="Game status">
            <div class="hud-heading">
              <span class="hud-index" id="hud-index">01</span>
              <span class="hud-title">/ NEURAL MATRIX</span>
            </div>

            <div class="hud-grid">
              <div class="hud-item">
                <span class="hud-label">INPUT REQUIRED</span>
                <span class="hud-value" id="input-required">AWAITING INPUT</span>
              </div>

              <div class="hud-item">
                <span class="hud-label">SIGNAL STATUS</span>
                <span class="hud-value cyan" id="signal-status">STANDBY</span>
              </div>

              <div class="hud-item">
                <span class="hud-label">SECURITY LEVEL</span>
                <span class="hud-value cyan" id="security-level">01 / 07</span>
              </div>

              <div class="hud-item">
                <span class="hud-label">ACCESS MODE</span>
                <span class="hud-value" id="access-mode">STANDBY</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- TRIANGLE MATRIX
               This stage contains the interactive nodes and decorative
               interface details. The JavaScript generates the triangle
               buttons and manages their sequence-based interaction. -->
          <div class="matrix-stage" id="matrix-stage">
            <div class="stage-grid" aria-hidden="true"></div>
            <div class="stage-corner corner-tl" aria-hidden="true"></div>
            <div class="stage-corner corner-tr" aria-hidden="true"></div>
            <div class="stage-corner corner-bl" aria-hidden="true"></div>
            <div class="stage-corner corner-br" aria-hidden="true"></div>

            <div class="matrix" id="matrix" aria-label="Triangle nodes"></div>

            <!-- WRONG-ANSWER FEEDBACK
                 This overlay communicates an incorrect sequence.
                 The alert role and assertive live region are intended
                 to make important error feedback available to
                 assistive technologies as well as visually. -->
            <div class="error-overlay" id="error-overlay" hidden>
              <div class="error-box" role="alert" aria-live="assertive">
                <span class="error-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
                <div class="error-title">ACCESS LOCKED</div>
                <div class="error-description">SEQUENCE MISMATCH</div>
                <div class="error-subtext">REPLAYING SIGNAL...</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- FINAL SUCCESS FEEDBACK
                 This overlay communicates completion after the player
                 successfully finishes the required security levels.
                 The success message provides a clear end point for
                 the memory-game interaction. -->
            <div class="success-overlay" id="success-overlay" hidden>
              <div class="success-box">
                <div class="success-icon" aria-hidden="true">✓</div>
                <p class="success-eyebrow">ALL SECURITY LEVELS VERIFIED</p>
                <h2>SYSTEM <span>UNLOCKED</span></h2>
                <p class="success-description">MEMORY SEQUENCE COMPLETE.</p>
                <div class="success-line"></div>
                <p class="success-footer">ACCESS GRANTED // TP-07</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- This label communicates the current stage instruction.
                 It supports the visual status indicators around the
                 matrix and helps explain what the player should do. -->
            <div class="stage-bottom-label">
              <span class="tiny-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span id="stage-instruction">SYSTEM IN STANDBY</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="matrix-footer">
            <span>MEMORY TEST // TP-07</span>
            <span>SECURE CONNECTION // ESTABLISHED</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- RIGHT PANEL: CONTROLS
             The aside groups the mission information, progress display
             and restart/reset controls. Separating these controls from
             the triangle matrix helps distinguish game input from
             system management actions. -->
        <aside class="mission-panel" aria-label="Memory protocol controls">
          <div class="mission-heading">
            <span class="heading-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <h2>MEMORY PROTOCOL</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="level-display">
            <span class="label">LEVEL</span>
            <span class="level-number" id="level-count">01 / 07</span>
          </div>

          <!-- Dynamic status text explains the current game state
               and provides instructions for the next action. -->
          <div class="signal-message">
            <p class="signal-title" id="matrix-status">SYSTEM STANDBY.</p>
            <p class="signal-description" id="instruction">
              UNLOCK THE SYSTEM TO BEGIN.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- PROGRESS INDICATOR
               The progress bar shows advancement through the seven
               levels. Its ARIA attributes describe the minimum,
               maximum and current progress values. -->
          <div class="level-progress-section">
            <div class="progress-heading">
              <span>LEVEL PROGRESS</span>
              <span id="level-progress">00 / 07</span>
            </div>

            <div
              class="progress-track"
              id="progress-track"
              role="progressbar"
              aria-label="Completed levels"
              aria-valuemin="0"
              aria-valuemax="7"
              aria-valuenow="0"
            >
              <div class="progress-fill" id="progress-fill"></div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- INTERACTION CONTROLS
               Restart and reset provide ways to repeat or leave the
               current test. The separate unlock button offers another
               entry point into the game. Each action uses a button
               element to support standard interactive behaviour. -->
          <div class="mission-actions">
            <button class="text-button restart-button" id="start-button" type="button">
              RESTART TEST <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
            </button>

            <button class="text-button reset-button" id="reset-button" type="button">
              RESET PROTOCOL
            </button>

            <button class="unlock-game-button" id="unlock-game-button" type="button">
              UNLOCK <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </button>
          </div>

          <!-- This live region is available for feedback updates
               from JavaScript without requiring a page reload. -->
          <p class="feedback-message" id="feedback" aria-live="polite">
            SYSTEM READY.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <!-- FOOTER
           The footer reinforces the fictional identity of the game
           and provides a consistent ending to the interface layout. -->
      <footer class="page-footer">
        <span>TP-07 // MEMORY TEST</span>
        <span>INTERACTIVE SYSTEMS DIVISION</span>
      </footer>
    </section>
  </main>
</body>
</html>